<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Khani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1650–1707</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Khani was a Kurdish poet, scholar and mystic from the Hakkari region who settled at Bayazid and wrote in Kurmanji Kurdish. His verse epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-and-zin">Mem and Zin</a>, completed in 1692, retells a tragic love story set at the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Bohtan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Cizre</a>. Its preface calls on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> to unite under a ruler of their own, and it is regarded as the national epic of Kurdish literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-khani.jpg" alt="Ahmad Khani" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diyar Muhammed · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Kurdish_poet_and_writer_Ahmadi_Xani_in_Sulaymaniyah,_Kurdistan,_Iraq.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
